Step 0 rules out an honest Track A claim for the triaged dense planted-STS idea: the paper proves existence, not distributional search hardness, and its proof explicitly reduces the approximate search to hypergraph matching/fractional matching. Dense random hosts also admit many witnesses, so planting one does not make recovery hard.

A better paper-native candidate is the paper’s Proposition 1.7 obstruction: a hidden four-part vertex partition is an exact parity certificate that no STS exists. I’m testing whether its recovery can support Track B (generic reconstruction is mechanical, while the parity/coset structure can be expressed compactly) without collapsing into a cheap visible coloring.
