codex
Implemented a Track B generator based on the paper’s Proposition 1.7 parity obstruction ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.15909)).

Files:

- [gen_2310_15909.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2310.15909/gen_2310_15909.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2310.15909/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2310.15909/README.md)
- [bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2310.15909/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2310.15909/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2310.15909/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Local results:

- G1–G8 pass.
- Guessing: 0/200,000 over a 443-bit structure-aware space.
- Five attacks: 0/8 each.
- Track B reference algorithm: 3,884 operations; succeeds 8/8.
- Answer: 1,043 characters, 229 atoms.
- Compact route: 228 modular multiplications.
- Canonical-key audit: 80/80 transformations, including affine residue symmetries.

The result is not yet shippable: every official bare/hinted/placebo oracle request returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded`. Consequently G9 remains pending, `all_passed` is false, and submission validation correctly fails only G9. No hardness failure was fabricated from those API errors, and the paper was not rejected.
