240 README.md
     1	# Steiner triple-system parity certificates (arXiv:2310.15909)
     2	
     3	Status: the generator and all local gates pass, but this result is **not yet
     4	shippable**. The required OpenRouter hardening and G9 runs were attempted by the
     5	official script and every request returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded`; errors
     6	are not model failures. The script-owned error transcripts are retained so the
     7	blocker is auditable.
     8	
     9	| profile field | value |
    10	|---|---|
    11	| Track | B — no-tool compression |
    12	| Native domain | combinatorics |
    13	| Object regime | finite discrete |
    14	| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
    15	| Certificate form | integer tuple (a natural four-set partition) |
    16	| Intuition | change of variables: translate public residues and recognize quartic cosets |
    17	| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
    18	
    19	## Problem and trust model
    20	
    21	[Hyunwoo Lee, *Towards a high-dimensional Dirac's theorem*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.15909)
    22	studies Steiner triple systems (STSs) inside 3-uniform hypergraphs. An STS is a
    23	set of triples that covers each vertex pair exactly once. This family asks for
    24	a four-part partition certifying that no STS exists. `verify` checks the
    25	partition, every hyperedge's part pattern, and the final odd parity count using
    26	integers only; it does not read the planted answer.
    27	
    28	The construction is exactly the obstruction in Proposition 1.7 (Section 1.4),
    29	followed by safe random edge deletion. `V0` is even and `V1,V2,V3` are odd.
    30	The allowed edge types force a certain odd number of pairs between the three
    31	odd parts to be covered by triples contributing two such pairs each, an
    32	impossibility. Sampling the partition first and then sampling all retained
    33	edges uniformly from the same allowed set gives a certificate by construction.
    34	
    35	This is Track B. Theorem 1.2/Corollary 1.9 only prove asymptotic existence above
    36	the paper's codegree threshold; they do not prove that a random planted search
    37	distribution is hard. Section 3 also gives a max-flow/min-cut construction for
    38	the fractional relaxation, and Sections 4–6 use matching and absorption. Those
    39	facts rule out the proposed Track A story. Here the efficient reference method
    40	computes one quartic character per residue in `O(n log n + |E|)`. At `n=229` it
    41	used 2,052 modular multiplications, 1,832 edge checks, and under 0.01 seconds.
    42	The compact route notices the primitive-root orbit and uses 228 modular
    43	multiplications. The former is easy for a program but not a realistic unaided
    44	calculation; the latter fits the 300-operation cap if the structure is seen.
    45	
    46	## Worked demo
    47	
    48	This is the complete `demo`, seed 0. A person can solve it on paper: start from
    49	the marked center residue 8, repeatedly multiply nonzero differences by the
    50	displayed primitive root 2 modulo 13, and group orbit positions modulo 4.
    51	
    52	```text
    53	STEINER-TRIPLE-SYSTEM PARITY CERTIFICATE
    54	
    55	A 3-uniform hypergraph has vertices 0,...,n-1 and hyperedges that are unordered
    56	triples of distinct vertices. A Steiner triple system (STS) inside it would be
    57	a set of hyperedges in which every unordered pair of distinct vertices occurs
    58	in exactly one selected triple.
    59	
    60	Certify that the displayed hypergraph has no STS by finding an ordered
    61	partition [V0,V1,V2,V3]. Every vertex must occur exactly once. Order inside a
    62	part is irrelevant and repetitions are forbidden.
    63	The required part sizes are [4,3,3,3]; V0 must contain both marked vertices 8
    64	and 1.
    65	Every displayed hyperedge must avoid all three forbidden color patterns:
    66	  (i) three vertices in V0;
    67	  (ii) one vertex in V0 and two vertices in the same Vi for i=1,2,3;
    68	  (iii) one vertex in each of V1,V2,V3.
    69	Here the color of a vertex is the index of the part containing it.
    70	These conditions are an exact parity certificate: V0 is even and the other
    71	parts are odd, and the remaining cross-pairs would have to be covered two at a
    72	time by any alleged STS.
    73	
    74	n = p = 13; g = 2
    75	required sizes = [4,3,3,3]
    76	marked vertices in V0 = 8, 1
    77	vertex:residue =
    78	0:1 1:10 2:9 3:5 4:11 5:2 6:3 7:7 8:8 9:4 10:0 11:12 12:6
